<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Melkote</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Melukote</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Melkote is a hill town in Mandya district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, and a major centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-vaishnavism">Sri Vaishnavism</a>. The philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a> lived there for about twelve years in the early 12th century, and its Cheluvanarayana Swamy temple and hilltop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoga">Yoga</a> Narasimha temple remain important places of pilgrimage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/melkote.jpg" alt="Melkote" width="320" height="333" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philanthropist 1 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melukotetemple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Melukote lies in Pandavapura taluk of Mandya district, built on a rocky range called Yadugiri, Yaadavagiri or Yadushaila that rises to 1,094 metres above sea level and overlooks the Cauvery valley. It is about 36 km north-west of Mandya and 51 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, and it is the only settlement of the district above 1,000 metres. The name comes from the temple of Narayanaswamy, built on the hillock and surrounded by a fort; mythological accounts also call the place Narayanadri, Vedadri, Yadavadri, Yathishaila and Tirunarayanapuram.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a>'s stay, large numbers of Iyengar Brahmins settled in the region, forming the Mandyam Iyengar community. Epigraphic evidence shows that the deity of the main temple was already a well-known object of worship before Ramanuja came. The temple enjoyed the patronage of the Vijayanagara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> kings; in 1614 Raja Wodeyar I of Mysore granted it a large estate, and a bas relief of him standing with folded hands is carved on a pillar of its nava-aranga. The fort on the hill was perhaps built in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> and Vijayanagara period.</p><p>The town remembers a massacre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, among them Mandyam Iyengars, by soldiers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>, who attacked the temple on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwali">Diwali</a> morning in the 1790s while the puja was going on. The people of Melukote stopped celebrating Diwali after it, and the day has remained one of mourning.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Cheluvanarayana Swamy temple owns three jewelled crowns: the Raja-mudi, the Krishnaraja-mudi given by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnaraja-wodeyar-iii">Krishnaraja Wodeyar III</a>, and the older Vairamudi of unknown origin. They are kept in the state treasury at Mandya and brought to the temple only for the Vairamudi festival, which has drawn up to 400,000 people. The temple is under the control of the Yadugiri Yathiraja Mutt.</p><p>Melukote is also home to the Academy of Sanskrit Research, whose library holds 11,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and 35,000 books, a Sanskrit college founded in 1854, and the Melkote Temple Wildlife Sanctuary, created on 17 June 1974 chiefly to protect wolves. The town is known for its handlooms, especially dhoties and sarees.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Melukote” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to tradition Vishnuvardhana himself turned to Vaishnavism under the influence of Ramanuja, the Sri Vaishnava philosopher who lived at <b>Melkote</b> in the Hoysala country, while Shaivism flourished too, and the Lingayat movement spread among the population in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
